    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "Neri became the fastest apprentice in the city courier house by memorizing signs. The fish market had a blue board shaped like a wave, the west gate used three tall strokes, and the magistrate's office began with a mark that resembled a hooked nose. Neri could match a sealed letter to fifty familiar boards without reading the smaller writing beneath them. The chief courier praised his remarkable visual memory and gave him the central route.\n\nThe method failed when the sign painter injured his hand. His daughter repainted the market boards in a narrower style, without the flourishes Neri had stored in memory. The west gate no longer looked like the west gate. Two shop names that had always seemed different now shared almost the same outline. Neri delivered a spice invoice to a candle maker and carried a summons past the courthouse twice. He spent that evening copying whole signs more carefully, convinced he simply needed sharper pictures in his head.\n\nA month later, winter rain blurred several boards. Neri could still recognize the blue fish, but not a temporary notice beside it announcing that deliveries had moved to the south hall. He guessed from the first large mark and joined the wrong queue. By noon, three carts blocked the market entrance. The chief courier took away the central route. Neri had memorized more signs than any apprentice, yet a new word, a new hand, or a missing ornament returned him to guessing.\n\nThe retired typesetter across the lane offered him a different exercise. She said a street name aloud and made Neri break it into its smallest sound movements. Then she placed letter tiles beneath those sounds and asked what changed when one sound was replaced. Neri disliked the slowness. He already knew the name as a single shape. But after several evenings, he could hear where the spoken word turned and see which marks consistently carried those turns. The letters stopped being decorative edges around a picture.\n\nThe typesetter then mixed familiar and invented shop names in plain black print. Neri could not retrieve the new names from memory because he had never seen them. He sounded through one, joined the parts, and reached a meaning he recognized from speech. The first attempts were laborious. Repeated encounters became quicker until several words seemed to arrive at once, without conscious sounding. When the sign painter returned with yet another style, Neri noticed the difference but did not lose the city.\n\nWhat changed was not the size of Neri's picture collection. Written forms had become bonded to their pronunciations and meanings through orthographic mapping. In alphabetic reading, this process draws on awareness of speech sounds and knowledge of how letters represent them; repeated successful connections allow words to be stored for rapid recognition. The familiar word eventually looks effortless, but the memory is supported by an ordered link among spelling, sound, and meaning, not by a photograph of its outline.",
    "storyBodyZh": "内里靠记招牌，成了城里信使馆跑得最快的学徒。鱼市是一块像波浪的蓝牌，西门开头有三道高高的竖笔，执政官办公室的第一个记号像一只弯鼻子。内里不用读下面的小字，就能把密封信件送到五十块熟悉的牌子前。总信使夸他有惊人的图像记忆，把最重要的中央路线交给了他。\n\n招牌画师伤了手以后，这个办法开始失灵。画师的女儿用更窄、更少花饰的笔法重画市场招牌，西门忽然不再像西门；两个从前轮廓完全不同的店名，现在看起来几乎一样。内里把香料账单送进蜡烛铺，又两次从法院门前带着传票走过。那晚，他更认真地临摹整块招牌，认定自己只是需要把脑中的图画记得更清楚。\n\n一个月后，冬雨冲淡了几块木牌。内里仍认得蓝色的鱼，却读不出旁边临时加上的通知：货物改送南厅。他根据第一个大字猜了方向，排进错误队伍。中午前，三辆货车堵住了市场入口，总信使收回了他的中央路线。内里记住的招牌比任何学徒都多，可一遇到新词、新笔迹或少掉的花饰，他便重新回到猜测。\n\n街对面的退休排字工给了他另一种练习。她先念出一个街名，让内里把它拆成最小的声音变化，再把字母木块排到这些声音下面，问他替换一个声音时，哪些字母也要跟着改变。内里嫌这种做法太慢，因为他早已把街名记成一个完整形状。几个晚上以后，他却开始听见口语中的转折，也看见哪些记号总在承担同一种声音。字母不再只是围在图画边缘的装饰。\n\n排字工把熟悉店名和编造出来的新店名混在一起，全部用最普通的黑字印出。新词从未见过，内里无法从记忆里直接取出。他逐个辨认声音，再把它们合起来，终于抵达一个自己在口语中认识的意思。最初几次十分费力，重复成功以后，若干词开始像同时出现一样，不必再刻意拼读。招牌画师后来换了另一种笔法，内里看得出变化，却没有再次失去整座城市。\n\n改变的不是内里收藏了更多词语图画，而是书写形式通过正字法映射，与发音和意义建立了稳定连接。在拼音文字阅读中，这个过程依赖对语音成分的觉察，以及对字母如何表示声音的认识；一次次成功连接，会让词语逐渐进入快速识别的长期记忆。熟词后来似乎可以毫不费力地被认出，但支撑这种熟练的不是轮廓照片，而是拼写、声音与意义之间有秩序的联系。",
    "explanation": "Orthographic mapping is the process through which a written word becomes securely linked in memory to its pronunciation and meaning. In alphabetic systems, phonemic awareness and letter-sound knowledge allow the reader to connect the sequence of letters with the sequence of sounds in a known spoken word.\n\nIt is not simple visual memorization. Repeated decoding strengthens the bond until recognition becomes fast and apparently immediate, while still being supported by the internal structure of the word.",
