    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "A traveller named Sen entered a city where every gate opened onto another district of libraries. He bought the largest map at the east gate and copied its street names onto his sleeves. Before sunset he had returned to the same fountain three times. The next morning he purchased a newer map with coloured roads. It carried him farther, but at an unlabelled alley he followed a confident merchant and arrived at the fountain again. Sen concluded that the city contained too little information and began collecting guidebooks.\n\nAn elderly librarian watched him fill a suitcase with maps. She offered no better one. Instead, she handed him a thin notebook and asked for three lines each evening: where he intended to go, where he actually changed course, and what made him change his mind. Sen considered the task insulting. He needed knowledge about the city, not a diary of his confusion. Still, the librarian would lend him a rare northern map only after seven completed pages, so he agreed.\n\nThe first pages looked trivial. He had turned because a road was crowded, because a symbol was unfamiliar, because someone else walked quickly. On the fourth day, Sen used the coloured map and still became lost. His notes showed that he repeatedly treated wide roads as correct roads. On the fifth, he stopped at an unlabelled alley, noticed the familiar impulse to retreat, and wrote down his prediction before entering. The alley led directly behind the museum he had been seeking all week.\n\nSen then tried to improve too many things at once. He walked slowly, checked every sign twice, questioned every passer-by, and arrived nowhere before closing time. That evening, the three lines exposed a different mistake: a strategy that helped at difficult junctions had been applied to every step. The librarian asked him to mark only the moments when confidence was high but evidence was weak. The next day, he reserved his careful checks for those points and moved quickly elsewhere.\n\nSeveral weeks later, a child at the south gate asked Sen which map was best. He almost opened his suitcase. Then he asked where the child usually became lost. Together they walked one route, recording a guess before each difficult turn and checking it afterward. The child discovered that she ignored house numbers whenever shop signs were bright. Sen discovered that fatigue made him follow crowds. The city had not become simple; their searching had become visible to the people doing it.\n\nThe three-line notebook had cultivated metacognitive regulation: planning how to approach a task, monitoring one's understanding and strategy while acting, evaluating what happened, and adjusting the next attempt. It is not endless self-analysis. Its purpose is to make learning more controllable by noticing when a method is failing, when confidence is misleading, and when a useful strategy is being overused. Sen still carried maps, but he no longer mistook owning them for knowing how he was learning.",
