    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "Master Olan taught irrigation from a blackboard. His canal diagram was famous for its clarity: reservoir at the top, two gates in the middle, four fields below. Students copied every line and labelled the slope, channel, and outlet. At examination time, Lio could redraw it from memory with his eyes nearly closed. When asked why the lower field received less water, he repeated Olan's sentence about distance and friction. The answer earned a perfect mark.\n\nThe next week, Olan replaced the desks with a long sand table. He gave each group a bucket, wooden gates, stones, and four cloth squares representing fields. Lio rebuilt the blackboard canal exactly. When water was poured, it cut through one sandy wall, pooled behind a level section, and flooded the nearest cloth. Lio quickly made the channels deeper. More water rushed into the same field. He stared at the broken diagram and said the sand table was too imperfect to demonstrate the lesson.\n\nOlan did not correct the model. He asked every student to draw where the next cup of water would go before it was poured. Their predictions disagreed. One believed water used itself up as it travelled; another thought the widest channel always received most; Lio assumed the labelled lower field must eventually fill because that was what the board showed. They tested each prediction. Stones redirected the current, a gate changed pressure upstream, and a slight rise that no one had measured stopped an entire branch.\n\nThe class then built a second canal with the same amount of water but different slopes. Olan introduced a clear tube to help them compare heights and asked which observation their earlier ideas could not explain. Lio crossed out 'distance' in his notebook and replaced it with a set of relations among level, resistance, gate position, and channel shape. He still used Olan's diagram, but now as one case among several rather than a picture whose labels guaranteed the outcome.\n\nAt the village garden, the students encountered a blocked ditch after heavy rain. Lio did not immediately reproduce the school model. He asked gardeners where water had pooled, measured the ground, and invited them to predict what opening one gate would do upstream. Their first repair shifted the problem to another bed. They revised the channel together, using the failed attempt as information. By evening, the water reached all four plots without drowning the closest one.\n\nThe sand table had made learning constructive rather than merely reproductive. Constructivist learning holds that learners actively build and revise understanding by connecting new experience with prior ideas. Those prior ideas shape what they notice and can survive beneath correct words until prediction, evidence, dialogue, and guided activity make them available for revision. The teacher does not disappear; Olan designed the problem, supplied tools, asked questions, and introduced evidence. What he could not do was pour a finished model directly into Lio's mind.",
    "storyBodyZh": "奥兰师傅用黑板教授灌溉。他的水渠图以清楚闻名：上方一座蓄水池，中段两道闸门，下方四块田。学生抄下每一根线，标出坡度、渠道和出口。考试时，里奥几乎闭着眼也能完整重画。被问到为什么低处田地得到的水较少，他便复述奥兰关于距离与摩擦的句子，拿到满分。\n\n下一周，奥兰撤走课桌，换成一张长长沙盘，给每组一桶水、木闸、石头和四块代表田地的布。里奥完全照着黑板重建水渠。水一倒下去，便冲破一侧沙墙，在一段水平渠道后积住，又淹没最近的布块。里奥赶紧把渠道挖深，更多水反而冲向同一块田。他看着破掉的图形，认定沙盘太不完美，根本无法演示课程。\n\n奥兰没有替他修模型，只让每个人在下一杯水倒下前先画出它会去哪里。学生的预测互不相同。有人以为水在路上会被用完，有人认为最宽渠道总能得到最多水，里奥则相信标着“低处”的田最后一定会灌满，因为黑板就是这样画的。他们逐一检验。石头改变水向，闸门影响上游，一点没人量过的小隆起就让整条支渠停住。\n\n全班随后用同样多的水，按照不同坡度建第二套水渠。奥兰拿出透明软管帮助他们比较高度，又追问：哪一项观察是旧想法解释不了的？里奥划掉笔记里的“距离”，改写成水位、阻力、闸门位置与渠道形状之间的一组关系。他仍然使用奥兰的图，只是不再把它当成标签会自动兑现的答案，而是众多情形中的一个。\n\n学生来到村庄菜园时，正遇上暴雨后堵塞的水沟。里奥没有立刻复制学校模型，而是先问园丁水积在哪里，测量地面，再请大家预测打开某道闸会怎样影响上游。第一次修理把问题推去了另一块菜畦，他们便把失败当成信息，一起改动渠道。黄昏时，水终于到达四块田，又没有淹掉最近的一块。\n\n沙盘让学习从复制变成了建构。建构主义学习认为，学习者会把新经验与原有观念连接起来，主动建立并修正理解。旧观念会影响人看见什么，也可能藏在正确术语下面，直到预测、证据、对话和有引导的活动让它们接受检验。教师并没有消失：奥兰设计问题、提供工具、提出追问并引入证据；他唯一不能做的，是把一套完成的模型直接倒进里奥脑中。",
    "explanation": "Constructivist learning describes understanding as something learners actively build and revise through the interaction of prior knowledge, new experience, language, evidence, and social activity. Correct recall does not guarantee that the underlying model has changed.\n\nIt does not require leaving learners to discover everything alone. Well-designed instruction provides structure, tasks, tools, explanations, questions, and feedback that make existing ideas visible and help learners reorganize them.",
    "explanationZh": "建构主义学习把理解视为学习者在原有知识、新经验、语言、证据和社会互动之间主动建立并修正的结果。能够正确复述，并不保证底层理解模型已经改变。\n\n它不等于让学习者独自发现一切。良好教学仍会提供结构、任务、工具、解释、问题和反馈，使原有观念变得可见，并帮助学习者重新组织它们。"
