    "explanation": "Moral luck describes cases in which factors beyond an agent's control affect moral judgement, despite the intuition that people should be assessed only for what they control. Philosophers discuss resultant, circumstantial, constitutive, and causal forms of luck.\n\nThe problem does not imply that outcomes never matter or that responsibility disappears. It asks how judgement should distinguish agency and foresight from consequences, circumstances, character formation, and causal conditions not fully chosen by the person.",
