    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "As floodwater approached the valley, the council had to choose an evacuation road. Scholar Pel presented a map and three sentences: every road marked blue remains above the hundred-year flood; the eastern road is marked blue; therefore the eastern road will remain above the flood. If the two premises held, denying the conclusion would be impossible. The council admired the clean reasoning and sent the first wagons east.\n\nThe wagons returned with water at their axles. Pel's form had not broken. The map legend had. Blue lines indicated roads maintained by the crown, not elevation, and the first premise came from a clerk who had mistaken colour for height. The argument had carried the council flawlessly from what it accepted to what followed. It could not inspect the world and certify its own starting points.\n\nAn angry councillor declared formal reasoning useless and proposed the western road because his grandfather had once travelled it safely. The western road was in fact dry, but his inference was weak: one old journey under unknown conditions did not establish safety during this flood. A true conclusion had been reached by reasoning that could easily have produced false ones in similar cases. Being right once did not repair the missing support.\n\nPel separated two questions on the board. First: does the conclusion follow necessarily from the premises? Second: are the premises actually true? Surveyors checked elevations, dates, and the meaning of map symbols. A correct survey with a missing inferential link would be no better than a perfect inference from fantasy alone. The council also searched for hidden premises, such as whether bridges counted as part of a road and whether the current flood could exceed the historical level. Logical structure and factual inquiry became complementary tasks.\n\nThey built a new argument from surveyed contours and inspected bridges. It pointed north, though the route was longer. Before departure, one member tried to refute it by imagining all its premises true and its conclusion false. She could not. Then the engineers tested each premise against measurements and current damage. The council no longer asked logic to manufacture facts or evidence to substitute for a connection among claims.\n\nPel had distinguished validity and soundness. A deductive argument is valid when no possible case makes all its premises true and its conclusion false; validity concerns the inferential form or relation, not whether the premises happen to be true. A sound argument is valid and has true premises, so its conclusion is true. Invalid reasoning may stumble onto a true conclusion, and valid reasoning may preserve a false starting point perfectly. The map taught the council to examine both the road from premises and the ground beneath them.",
