    "storyBody": "During the drought, Valen's canal carried enough water to serve either the grain mill or the hillside orchard each afternoon, not both. The mill needed a strong six-hour flow to turn its wheel. The orchard needed the same volume to keep young trees alive. The council called the river free and chose the mill because flour sales appeared in that week's ledger while unripe fruit did not.\n\nThe mill produced sacks of flour without anyone paying for the water. Councillors congratulated themselves on obtaining output at no resource cost. Three weeks later, orchard leaves browned and part of the future harvest died. Families bought fruit from another valley, seasonal workers lost employment, and bare soil washed toward the canal. The mill's choice had carried a cost even though no coin changed hands at the gate.\n\nAn accountant tried to total every possible thing the village might have done with water: washing streets, filling ponds, making bricks, growing reeds, and watering gardens. The list became enormous. Orchard keeper Sena objected that most options were not serious alternatives under current tools and needs. If the orchard was truly next best, its expected harvest and related benefits formed the comparison; brick ponds could not all be lost at once. The relevant comparison was the most valuable feasible option that would have been chosen next, not the sum of every imaginable use.\n\nThe following year, the council saved the orchard and reduced milling. Now flour had to be ground at a distant town. The same water generated a different sacrifice because harvest prices, grain stocks, tree maturity, and transport conditions had changed. Uncertainty required expected values rather than certainty about either harvest before the gate opened. No use possessed one permanent hidden price. The cost depended on what scarce resources could otherwise have achieved at the moment of choice.\n\nA proposal to split the water equally looked fair but failed both technologies: the wheel stalled under weak flow, and too little water reached the upper roots. The council redesigned the schedule instead, alternating full flows and investing in storage. That did not remove scarcity, but it changed the feasible alternatives and therefore changed what had to be forgone. Better infrastructure could lower a cost by making the next-best option less incompatible.\n\nValen had encountered opportunity cost: the value of the best alternative forgone when a choice uses scarce resources. It need not be a monetary payment, and it is not the sum of every rejected possibility. Opportunity cost makes choice visible by comparing what is selected with the next-best feasible use. River water could be free at the point of collection and still be costly, because every afternoon it flowed toward one future rather than another.",
