    "storyBody": "Merchant Arin placed his entire saffron harvest on the Swift Gull because it had the fastest captain and highest expected profit. The ship struck an uncharted reef, and one accident erased the year's capital. His adviser told him to spread the next cargo. Arin hired five vessels and congratulated himself on prudence. All five sailed in one convoy through the same narrow channel to the same buyer under contracts denominated in the same unstable currency.\n\nA regional storm closed the channel, the buyer failed, and the currency fell before any ship unloaded. Arin lost almost as completely as before. He had divided containers without dividing the causes of loss. Vessel count looked varied, but route, market, counterparty, timing, cargo, and currency exposures moved together. Five names on a manifest could still represent one concentrated bet.\n\nThe adviser mapped which returns tended to rise and fall together. The map used covariance rather than labels: two different businesses could fail together, while similar cargoes on genuinely separate routes might still respond differently under pressure. Some saffron went by the northern route, some later in the season, and some to buyers using different currencies. Arin invested part of his capital in grain storage and rope making, whose results depended on other conditions. He retained the Swift Gull for cargo where its speed mattered. The aim was not equal pieces or maximum variety but a combination whose imperfectly related outcomes reduced avoidable portfolio volatility.\n\nThe next year, a reef damaged one ship while strong grain prices offset part of the loss. Arin called the method a guarantee and began buying tiny shares in every venture offered. Fees rose, oversight weakened, and several apparently distinct companies all depended on the same shipping insurer. More holdings did not automatically improve the combination. Expected return, concentration, costs, liquidity, quality, and dependence still required judgement alongside co-movement.\n\nThen war closed every regional port. Grain, rope, spice, and ships all fell together as correlations rose during crisis. The portfolio lost money, though less than the original single cargo would have. Broad shocks could not be removed merely by combining local assets; spreading specific risks left systematic risk behind. Arin kept reserves and limits because uncertainty also included events absent from the historical map used to estimate relationships.\n\nArin had learned diversification: combining exposures whose returns are not perfectly positively related so that asset-specific losses can offset one another and total risk may fall for a given expected return. The benefit depends on weights, variance, covariance, and changing relationships, not the number of holdings alone. Diversification cannot guarantee profit or eliminate common shocks, and concentration may sometimes be deliberate. Five ships protected Arin only when they stopped carrying the same hidden voyage.",
