    "storyBody": "When Ordan's first river bridge shifted after an exceptional flood, the city blamed its builder. Their contract required the builder to repair ordinary wear, but the city had selected the route and withheld an old survey showing a migrating channel. The High Chamber ruled that the city must pay for relocation because the loss arose from a concealed site condition under its control, not ordinary maintenance. In closing, one judge also remarked that a three-arch stone bridge was the safest form for a serious city.\n\nTen years later, a rope bridge failed at a mountain crossing. A clerk opened the old judgment, underlined the praise of stone arches, and declared the new builder liable for choosing rope. Merchants liked the certainty of one sentence for every bridge. Judge Sela asked which question the earlier court had needed to answer. The dispute there concerned allocation of a concealed site risk under a particular contract. No party had contested rope, stone, or the number of arches. The memorable sentence had helped explain a preference; it had not decided the case.\n\nSela did not simply discard the old ruling. She compared the contracts, who controlled the surveys, the kind of failure, and the authority of the court. Where the material facts and issue matched, the earlier holding constrained later judges in the same hierarchy. Where the mountain bridge differed, the reasoning might still persuade without controlling the result. She required the clerk to state the governing proposition narrowly enough that its force came from what had been adjudicated, not from any attractive line copied from the page.\n\nA third dispute then matched the first almost exactly. The city again concealed a channel report and argued that a new panel should start afresh. Sela followed the earlier allocation. Builders had priced contracts, insurers had written policies, and citizens had arranged conduct around the court's announced rule. Treating like cases alike limited personal whim and made law more predictable. Consistency was not worship of age; it protected equality, reliance, and the accumulated work of prior reasoning.\n\nYears later, repeated floods and a new statute made part of the old rule unworkable. A higher chamber did not pretend the earlier words had never existed. It identified the holding, examined reliance, explained the changed legal setting, and openly replaced the rule for future disputes. Lower courts could distinguish genuinely different facts, while only an authorised court could overrule a binding decision. The chain held because each link disclosed whether it was following, distinguishing, criticising, or replacing what came before.\n\nThe bridge records expressed the doctrine of precedent: earlier judicial decisions guide later cases, with binding force depending on jurisdiction, hierarchy, and the issue actually decided, while other reasoning may carry persuasive weight. The related practice of standing by decided matters promotes consistency and reliance but is not an inexorable command. Material differences may justify distinction, and authorised courts may overrule with reasons. The second bridge was not bound by every sentence beneath the first; it was bound, where applicable, by the decision the first dispute had made necessary.",
    "storyBodyZh": "奥丹第一座河桥在一次异常洪水后移位，城市向建造者追责。合同要求建造者修复正常损耗，但路线由城市选定，城市还隐瞒一份显示河道迁移的旧测绘。最高审查庭裁定，损失来自城市控制并隐瞒的场地条件，而非普通维护，因此迁桥费用由城市承担。结尾时，一名法官还顺带写道，三拱石桥才是严肃城市最安全的样式。\n\n十年后，山区一座绳桥断裂。书记翻开旧裁决，在赞扬石拱的句子下画线，认定新建造者因采用绳索而负责。商人喜欢用一句话处理所有桥梁的确定感。法官塞拉却问，先前法院当时非回答不可的问题是什么。那场争议关乎特定合同下隐蔽场地风险的分配，没有一方争论绳索、石材或拱数。那句醒目的话解释了偏好，却没有决定案件。\n\n塞拉并未因此抛弃旧裁决。她比较合同、测绘控制者、故障类型和法院权威。若关键事实与争点相同，同一体系中上级法院先前作出的核心裁断会约束后来法官；若山桥情形不同，旧推理仍可具有说服力，却不控制结果。她要求书记把适用规则写得足够准确，使其力量来自真正经过裁判的事项，而非从纸页上抄下的漂亮句子。\n\n第三场争议几乎复制了第一场。城市再次隐瞒河道报告，并主张新合议庭应从零开始。塞拉沿用原先的风险分配。建造者据此给合同定价，保险人据此承保，市民也按法院宣布的规则安排事务。相同案件获得相同处理，能够限制个人任性并提高可预见性。一致不是崇拜年代，而是保护平等、信赖与既有推理积累。\n\n多年后，连续洪灾与新法规让旧规则的一部分无法运作。更高审查庭没有假装旧话从未存在，而是识别原裁断、审视人们的信赖、解释法律环境变化，并公开替换未来规则。下级法院可区分真正不同的事实，只有获授权的法院能够推翻有约束力的决定。链条之所以可靠，是因为每个环节都说明自己在遵循、区分、批评还是替代过去。\n\n桥梁档案体现的是判例原则：较早的司法决定会指导后来案件，其约束力取决于管辖、法院层级与真正经过裁定的争点，其他推理则可能具有说服力。遵循既决事项有助于一致与信赖，却不是不可摆脱的命令；重大事实差异可以支持区分，有权法院也可说明理由后推翻。第二座桥并不受第一座桥下每句话束缚；在条件适用时，它受约束的是第一场争议迫使法院作出的决定。",
    "explanation": "Precedent is the use of earlier judicial decisions to guide later cases. Binding effect depends on the court hierarchy, jurisdiction, material facts, and the holding needed to resolve the earlier issue; other statements may be persuasive rather than controlling.\n\nFollowing decided cases supports consistency, equality, and justified reliance. Courts may distinguish materially different cases, and courts with authority may overrule prior law through explicit reasoning rather than silently ignoring it. The form and strength of these practices vary across legal systems.",
    "explanationZh": "判例原则是用较早的司法决定指导后来案件。约束力取决于法院层级、管辖、关键事实，以及解决原争点所必需的核心裁断；其他陈述可能只有说服力。\n\n遵循既决案件有助于一致、平等与正当信赖。法院可区分事实存在重大差异的案件，有权法院也可通过明确论证推翻旧规则，而非默默忽略。这些实践的形式与效力在不同法律体系中并不相同。"
