    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "The Civic Hall had twelve marble steps and, behind the coal shed, a steep metal ramp built for one councillor who used a wheelchair. The ramp met the rule on opening day, then its key migrated to the caretaker's pocket. Delivery workers blocked it, rain made it slippery, and visitors using it entered beside rubbish bins. Architect Pel called it a special solution. Councillor Asha called it a second-class door. During an election queue, a parent lifted a pram up the marble steps while an older voter turned home without casting a ballot.\n\nAsha convened people with mobility, sensory, cognitive, and communication differences, along with children, older adults, workers, and staff. They did not offer one average body for Pel to design around. They mapped the full journey: finding the building, reaching it, entering, navigating, hearing announcements, reading forms, using counters and toilets, leaving during an emergency, and obtaining help without humiliation. A barrier at any link could make the nominally accessible room unusable.\n\nPel replaced the divided approach with a broad gradual route at the principal entrance. It offered firm slip-resistant surfaces, resting places, handrails, clear width, predictable edges, and doors operable with little force. High contrast and tactile cues supported wayfinding; signs used readable text and symbols; information came in more than one format; counters and seating offered choices; lighting and acoustics reduced avoidable strain. The design did not announce which feature belonged to which diagnosis. It simply gave more people a workable first path.\n\nOn reopening day, a wheelchair user entered independently, a courier moved a cart without lifting it, a child found the symbols, and a traveller with a temporary leg injury rested halfway. Pel declared that specialised support was now obsolete. Asha corrected him. One deaf visitor still required an interpreter for a complex hearing; another person needed a particular assistive device; an individual adjustment remained necessary for a staff member. Designing broadly reduced predictable exclusion but could not erase every specific requirement.\n\nThe first tactile map also confused people because Pel had copied a fashionable pattern without testing it. Users returned to evaluate prototypes, report friction, and change the details. Accessibility standards and rights obligations supplied minimum duties, not a ceiling for imagination. Cost, maintenance, emergency procedures, digital services, procurement, and future renovation were considered from the beginning. Inclusion worked better when it was a design condition and continuing process, rather than a late attachment requested by whoever was excluded first.\n\nThe shared entrance revealed universal design: creating products, environments, programmes, and services so they can be used by all people, to the greatest extent possible, without requiring adaptation or specialised design. It anticipates human diversity and often benefits many people, while still allowing assistive devices, reasonable accommodation, and individual support where needed. Pel's side ramp had asked one councillor to leave the public route. Asha changed the public route itself, and the city discovered how many citizens had been waiting behind the same steps.",
    "storyBodyZh": "市政厅正面有十二级大理石台阶，煤棚后方另建一条陡峭金属坡道，专供一名使用轮椅的议员。坡道在启用日符合规定，钥匙后来却总待在管理员口袋里；送货员会堵住它，下雨时表面打滑，来访者还得从垃圾桶旁进门。建筑师佩尔称它为特殊解决方案，阿莎议员却称它为二等入口。一次选举排队时，家长把婴儿车抬上大理石阶，一名年长选民则转身回家，没能投票。\n\n阿莎召集具有不同移动、感官、认知与沟通需要的人，也请来儿童、老年人、工人和职员。他们没有交给佩尔一具“平均身体”作为设计对象，而是绘制完整旅程：找到建筑、抵达、进入、辨路、听见通知、阅读表格、使用柜台与卫生间、紧急离开，以及不受羞辱地获得帮助。任何一环的障碍，都能让名义上可及的房间无法使用。\n\n佩尔把分裂的入口改成正门前宽阔平缓的共同路线，设置坚实防滑表面、休息处、扶手、足够宽度、可预测边缘和低力即可开启的门。高对比度与触觉提示帮助辨路，标牌结合易读文字和符号，信息提供多种格式，柜台与座椅有不同选择，灯光与声学也减少可避免负担。设计不再宣布哪项功能属于哪种诊断，只是让更多人一开始就有可行路径。\n\n重新开放那天，轮椅使用者独立进门，快递员不用抬起推车，孩子认出图形，腿部暂时受伤的旅客在半途休息。佩尔宣布专门支持从此已经过时，阿莎立刻纠正他。一名聋人参加复杂听证仍需要口译，另一人需要特定辅助设备，一名职员也仍需个别调整。广泛设计减少可预见排斥，却不可能抹去每项具体需要。\n\n第一版触觉地图也让人困惑，因为佩尔只是照搬流行图样，未让使用者测试。人们回来评估原型、报告摩擦并修改细节。可及性标准与权利义务提供最低责任，不是想象力的天花板。成本、维护、紧急程序、数字服务、采购与未来翻修从一开始就被考虑。当包容成为设计条件和持续过程，而不是等最先被排除者提出后才加上的附件时，它才更可靠。\n\n共同入口揭示的是通用设计：让产品、环境、方案与服务在最大可能程度上可由所有人使用，而不必另行改装或专门设计。它预先考虑人的多样性，常使许多人一同受益，同时仍为必要的辅助设备、合理便利与个别支持留下位置。佩尔的侧边坡道曾要求一名议员离开公共路线；阿莎改变了公共路线本身，城市这才发现，原来有那么多市民一直被同一排台阶挡在后面。",
    "explanation": "Universal design makes products, environments, programmes, and services usable by the widest range of people from the outset, to the greatest extent possible.\n\nIt anticipates diversity and reduces predictable barriers, but does not eliminate the need for accessibility standards, assistive technology, reasonable accommodation, participation, or individual support.",
