    "storyBody": "Guide Sorin owned the finest northern map in the province. When traveller Elia arrived unable to cross open bridges after a storm, he selected a proven mountain route, assigned daily crossings, and explained every contour with precision. Elia nodded through each meeting and completed none of the journeys. Sorin privately called her resistant. At their fourth appointment she said she was leaving: the destination she cared about was her sister's wedding in the east, while every task on his northern map seemed designed for someone else's life.\n\nSorin put away the map and asked three neglected questions. What change did Elia actually seek? Which activities could plausibly move toward it? What kind of working relationship would let them speak honestly when fear, disagreement, or shame entered the room? They agreed on the wedding as a meaningful goal, selected graded practice connected to the real journey, and discussed why each step might help. Elia could question a task without rejecting all help; Sorin could offer expertise without owning the destination.\n\nTheir agreement did not remove difficulty. Sorin scheduled a crowded bridge too early and interpreted Elia's silence as consent. She missed the next meeting. Instead of labelling the absence, he invited discussion of the rupture. Elia said his urgency had made her feel examined rather than accompanied. Sorin acknowledged the mismatch, clarified what he had been trying to do, and changed the sequence with her. Repair strengthened their ability to notice future strain; politeness had previously hidden it.\n\nWarmth alone was not enough. A guide who offered comfort but no useful method would not necessarily help Elia cross, and a shared goal could still be inappropriate or unsafe. Sorin used relevant assessment, evidence, supervision, cultural humility, and referral when needs exceeded his competence. He monitored progress and adverse effects rather than assuming a good relationship guaranteed benefit. Collaboration could support progress, while early progress could also make collaboration easier; their association did not prove one simple causal direction. Elia's view mattered especially, because a guide could believe trust existed while the traveller experienced pressure.\n\nNor did trust turn the relationship into friendship. Boundaries, confidentiality and its limits, roles, time, consent, records, and conflicts of interest remained explicit. Sorin did not ask Elia to protect his feelings or make his personal needs part of her journey. When she chose a different route, he explored the reasons without punishment. Their bond supported difficult work because it held respect and reliability, not because professional responsibility had disappeared.\n\nThe eastern journey revealed the therapeutic alliance: the collaborative working relationship formed through agreement on the goals of therapy, agreement on the tasks used to pursue them, and a bond of trust and respect. Its quality is associated with outcomes across approaches, but it is not friendship, a guarantee of success, or a substitute for competent methods and safety. Sorin's northern map had been excellent. It became useful only after guide and traveller could name the same destination, test the route together, and repair the road between them when it cracked.",
