    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "At Lantern Market, small purses disappeared every seventh-day evening near the eastern arcade. Captain Pell doubled random patrols and arrested more people for unrelated disorder, yet the same theft pattern continued. He demanded harsher character judgments about the district. Analyst Sera instead marked the exact times, paths, targets, transactions, guardians, exits, crowding, and reporting delays. The losses clustered where a blind turn met an unstaffed payment queue and customers had to expose open bags while both hands were occupied.\n\nThe market changed the immediate setting. Stalls were rearranged to improve natural visibility, the queue no longer crossed a hidden corner, secure storage became available, payment handling required less exposure, staff roles and reporting were clear, and confusing rules were simplified. These changes altered opportunity by increasing effort or perceived risk, reducing expected reward, reducing provocation, and removing excuses in ways tailored to that specific problem. They did not require discovering or transforming every possible offender before preventing the next event.\n\nPell declared the district cured after reports fell. Sera checked whether people had stopped reporting, whether theft moved to another entrance, time, target, method, or nearby market, which groups were affected, and whether benefits spread beyond the treated corner. Some displacement was possible but not automatic; changes could also produce diffusion of benefit when uncertainty about controls affected nearby choices. Counts were compared with suitable baselines and visitor volume, while residents and workers described consequences the incident ledger missed.\n\nOne proposed measure would have searched every young person entering the arcade. Sera rejected a broad intrusive response unsupported by the specific pattern. Prevention could create surveillance, privacy loss, stigma, inaccessible barriers, racial or class discrimination, hostile space, or exclusion of legitimate users. Measures therefore required proportionality, legality, evidence, accessibility, community participation, data safeguards, and review. Making a place harder to offend in should not make it impossible for some citizens to belong there.\n\nThe market also refused to treat opportunity reduction as a complete theory of crime or justice. Housing, inequality, trauma, exploitation, markets for stolen goods, policing legitimacy, and social services could require broader action. Victims were not blamed for carrying purses, and reduced opportunity did not determine guilt or replace fair investigation. The approach addressed one preventable event pattern while other institutions addressed causes and harms at other levels.\n\nThe altered corner revealed situational crime prevention: reducing specific crime opportunities by systematically changing the immediate environment or management to increase effort and risk, reduce rewards and provocations, or remove excuses. It is problem-specific and should be tested for displacement, benefit, equity, and unintended harm. Pell had searched the whole district for worse people. Sera studied one repeated situation closely enough to change what it invited, concealed, rewarded, and allowed.",
