    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "Meren's east road had become a daily queue, so Mayor Rusk added two lanes and promised ten years of free movement. On opening morning, carts and coaches crossed the city in half the old time. Rusk placed the saved minutes in a glass case at city hall. Planner Asha asked him to leave room for later measurements. Within months, the morning queue returned. The mayor accused the engineers of miscounting capacity, but the lanes were carrying far more vehicles than before. The road had not failed to accept traffic; it had changed the choices that produced traffic.\n\nAsha followed individual journeys. Drivers who had used side streets returned to the faster road. Some left at the busiest hour instead of travelling early. Former bus passengers began driving because the time advantage had shifted, and households that had postponed optional trips now made them. These were not one identical kind of journey. Some traffic had moved from another route, time, or mode; some trips had previously been suppressed by cost and delay. A snapshot of the widened road could not show which source had filled each new lane.\n\nThe slower change appeared beyond the kerb. Warehouses moved toward cheaper eastern land, shops surrounded themselves with parking, and new homes rose farther from schools and work. Destinations that had once seemed inconvenient became normal, while walking and frequent bus service became harder to sustain across the spreading city. Each household's decision made sense under the new conditions. Together, those decisions lengthened journeys and made owning a vehicle more useful, feeding traffic that the original forecast had treated as fixed.\n\nRusk concluded that extra road space must therefore be worthless. Asha disagreed. A safer junction, a missing rural link, reliable freight access, emergency resilience, or relief at a tightly constrained bottleneck could create real benefits. Nor was every newly made journey a social loss; access itself had value. The error was promising that capacity would preserve its first-day speed without estimating how routes, departure times, modes, trip frequency, destinations, and land use would respond in the short and long run.\n\nThe council stopped judging success by vehicle speed alone. It compared how many people and goods reached useful destinations, how reliable the whole journey was, who gained or lost access, and what happened to safety, emissions, neighbourhoods, and public cost. Road changes were tested alongside frequent transit, walking and cycling connections, parking and pricing choices, freight management, and land-use planning. Comparison corridors and updated forecasts helped separate the capacity response from population, income, fuel-price, and wider policy changes. Measurements continued after opening, so policy could adjust when behaviour differed from forecasts rather than calling every returning queue a surprise.\n\nThe road that filled its own empty space revealed induced demand: when travel becomes faster, cheaper, or easier, people and organisations alter routes, times, modes, trip frequency, destinations, and eventually land use, producing more use of the expanded capacity than a fixed-demand forecast predicts. Some of the increase is diverted or rescheduled travel, some is previously suppressed or newly generated travel, and some develops gradually through location choices. Rusk had built lanes for yesterday's journeys. Asha learned to forecast the journeys that the lanes themselves would help make reasonable tomorrow.",
    "storyBodyZh": "梅伦城东路每天排成长队，拉斯克市长于是增加两条车道，并承诺十年畅行。开通清晨，货车与客车穿城时间只有过去一半。拉斯克把节省下来的分钟写进玻璃柜，摆在市政厅里。规划师阿莎请他为后续测量留些空位。几个月后，早高峰队伍重新出现。市长责怪工程师算错容量，可车道实际承载的车辆远多于从前。道路不是没有接住交通，而是改变了产生交通的选择。\n\n阿莎逐一追踪出行。曾走小路的司机回到更快的主路；一些人不再提早出门，而在最拥挤时段上路；原先乘公交的人因为时间优势变化开始开车；过去因成本与延误而搁置可选行程的家庭，也把它们重新排进生活。这些并非同一种旅程。有的交通从其他路线、时段或方式转来，有的是此前被压下的出行。只看拓宽后的路面，无法知道每条新车道被哪一种来源填满。\n\n更慢的变化出现在路缘之外。仓库迁向东部廉价土地，商店被停车场包围，新住宅建在离学校和工作更远的地方。过去显得不便的目的地变得寻常，步行和高频公交却难以覆盖不断摊开的城市。每个家庭在新条件下的决定都有道理；这些决定合在一起，却拉长了路程，使拥有车辆更有用，也不断喂养原预测中被当成固定不变的交通量。\n\n拉斯克因此断定，增加道路空间毫无价值。阿莎并不同意。更安全的路口、缺失的乡村连接、可靠货运通道、紧急韧性，或极端瓶颈的缓解，都可能带来真实收益。新发生的旅程也不全是社会损失，可达性本身具有价值。错误在于承诺新增容量会永远保留开通首日的速度，却不估计路线、出发时间、交通方式、出行频率、目的地与土地利用在短期和长期如何回应。\n\n议会不再只用车速判断成功。它比较有多少人与货物可靠抵达有用目的地，谁获得或失去可达性，以及安全、排放、社区和公共成本发生了什么。道路改变要同高频公共交通、步行与骑行连接、停车和定价选择、货运管理及土地规划一起检验。比较走廊与更新预测帮助区分容量回应和人口、收入、燃料价格及更广政策变化。开通后的测量持续进行，当行为偏离预测时就调整政策，而不是把每次重新出现的队伍都称为意外。\n\n这条自己填满空处的路揭示的是诱发需求：当出行变得更快、更便宜或更容易，人和组织会改变路线、时段、方式、频率、目的地，最终还会改变区位与土地利用，使扩容后的使用量超过固定需求预测。有些增量来自改道或改时，有些来自过去受抑制或新生成的出行，还有一些通过区位选择逐渐形成。拉斯克为昨天的旅程修建车道；阿莎则学会预测这些车道会让哪些明日旅程变得合理。",
    "explanation": "Induced demand occurs when improved travel conditions change route, time, mode, frequency, destination, or location choices, causing expanded capacity to attract more use than fixed-demand forecasts expect.\n\nIt does not mean every capacity project or added trip lacks value. Good appraisal distinguishes sources and time scales, tests land-use and modal responses, measures access as well as speed, and compares road expansion with complementary or alternative policies.",
    "explanationZh": "诱发需求发生在出行条件改善改变路线、时段、方式、频率、目的地或区位选择时，使扩建容量吸引的使用量超过固定需求预测。\n\n它不意味着每项扩容工程或新增旅程都没有价值。良好评估会区分增量来源与时间尺度，检验土地和方式变化，同时衡量可达性与速度，并比较道路扩建和其他配套或替代政策。"
